Task 1: Verify KubeVirt Components
Subtask 1.1: Check OpenShift Virtualization Operator Status
First, let's verify that the OpenShift Virtualization operator is properly installed and running.

Login to your OpenShift cluster using the provided credentials:
oc login --token=<your-token> --server=<your-server-url>
Check the OpenShift Virtualization operator status:
oc get csv -n openshift-cnv
Expected output should show the ClusterServiceVersion (CSV) in Succeeded phase:

NAME                                      DISPLAY                    VERSION   REPLACES   PHASE
kubevirt-hyperconverged-operator.v4.14   OpenShift Virtualization   4.14.0               Succeeded
Verify the operator deployment:
oc get deployment -n openshift-cnv
You should see deployments like:

hco-operator
hyperconverged-cluster-cli-download
virt-template-validator
Subtask 1.2: Examine KubeVirt Core Components
Check the HyperConverged custom resource:
oc get hyperconverged -n openshift-cnv
Get detailed information about the HyperConverged resource:
oc describe hyperconverged kubevirt-hyperconverged -n openshift-cnv
Look for the Status section to ensure all components are available.

Verify KubeVirt deployment:
oc get kubevirt -n openshift-cnv
Expected output:

NAME       AGE   PHASE
kubevirt   10m   Deployed
Subtask 1.3: Check KubeVirt API Server and Controllers
Verify the virt-api deployment (KubeVirt API server):
oc get deployment virt-api -n openshift-cnv
Check virt-api pods are running:
oc get pods -n openshift-cnv -l kubevirt.io=virt-api
Verify the virt-controller deployment:
oc get deployment virt-controller -n openshift-cnv
Check virt-controller pods:
oc get pods -n openshift-cnv -l kubevirt.io=virt-controller
Verify virt-handler daemonset (runs on each node):
oc get daemonset virt-handler -n openshift-cnv
Check virt-handler pods on all nodes:
oc get pods -n openshift-cnv -l kubevirt.io=virt-handler
Subtask 1.4: Validate Component Health
Check the logs of virt-api to ensure it's healthy:
oc logs deployment/virt-api -n openshift-cnv --tail=20
Check virt-controller logs:
oc logs deployment/virt-controller -n openshift-cnv --tail=20
Verify all pods are in Running state:
oc get pods -n openshift-cnv | grep -v Completed
Task 2: Configure Operator Permissions and SCCs
Subtask 2.1: Understand Security Context Constraints
Security Context Constraints (SCCs) control the security context under which pods run in OpenShift.

List all SCCs in the cluster:
oc get scc
Examine the kubevirt-controller SCC:
oc describe scc kubevirt-controller
Check the privileged SCC (used by some KubeVirt components):
oc describe scc privileged
Subtask 2.2: Verify Service Account Permissions
List service accounts in the openshift-cnv namespace:
oc get serviceaccounts -n openshift-cnv
Check the kubevirt-controller service account:
oc describe serviceaccount kubevirt-controller -n openshift-cnv
Verify SCC assignments for kubevirt-controller:
oc describe scc kubevirt-controller | grep Users
Subtask 2.3: Configure Additional Permissions (if needed)
Create a custom service account for VM management (optional):
oc create serviceaccount vm-manager -n openshift-cnv
Grant necessary permissions to the service account:
oc adm policy add-scc-to-user kubevirt-controller -z vm-manager -n openshift-cnv
Verify the permission assignment:
oc describe scc kubevirt-controller | grep "system:serviceaccount:openshift-cnv:vm-manager"
Subtask 2.4: Configure Node-Level Permissions
Check if nodes have the required labels for virtualization:
oc get nodes -l cpumanager=true
Verify that virt-handler has proper node access:
oc get pods -n openshift-cnv -l kubevirt.io=virt-handler -o wide
Check cluster role bindings for KubeVirt:
oc get clusterrolebinding | grep kubevirt
Task 3: Validate Proper Installation and Permissions
Subtask 3.1: Test API Connectivity
Test KubeVirt API accessibility:
oc api-resources | grep kubevirt
Expected output should include resources like:

virtualmachines
virtualmachineinstances
virtualmachineinstancereplicasets
Test creating a simple VirtualMachine resource (dry-run):
cat << EOF | oc apply --dry-run=client -f -
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: test-vm
  namespace: openshift-cnv
spec:
  running: false
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: test-vm
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 1
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: containerdisk
        machine:
          type: pc-q35-rhel8.4.0
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 1Gi
      volumes:
      - containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/cirros-container-disk-demo
        name: containerdisk
EOF
Subtask 3.2: Validate Component Communication
Check if virt-api can communicate with the Kubernetes API:
oc logs deployment/virt-api -n openshift-cnv | grep -i "successfully\|ready\|started" | tail -5
Verify virt-controller is processing events:
oc logs deployment/virt-controller -n openshift-cnv | grep -i "controller\|started" | tail -5
Test webhook configurations:
oc get validatingwebhookconfigurations | grep virt
oc get mutatingwebhookconfigurations | grep virt
Subtask 3.3: Perform End-to-End Validation
Create a test namespace for validation:
oc create namespace kubevirt-test
Create a simple test VM:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: validation-vm
  namespace: kubevirt-test
spec:
  running: false
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: validation-vm
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 1
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: containerdisk
        machine:
          type: pc-q35-rhel8.4.0
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 512Mi
      volumes:
      - containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/cirros-container-disk-demo
        name: containerdisk
EOF
Verify the VM was created successfully:
oc get vm validation-vm -n kubevirt-test
Start the VM to test full functionality:
oc patch vm validation-vm -n kubevirt-test --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":true}}'
Check if the VM instance is created:
oc get vmi validation-vm -n kubevirt-test
Monitor the VM startup process:
oc describe vmi validation-vm -n kubevirt-test
Subtask 3.4: Clean Up Test Resources
Stop and delete the test VM:
oc patch vm validation-vm -n kubevirt-test --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":false}}'
oc delete vm validation-vm -n kubevirt-test
Delete the test namespace:
oc delete namespace kubevirt-test
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Pods Stuck in Pending State
If KubeVirt pods are stuck in pending state:

oc describe pod <pod-name> -n openshift-cnv
Check for:

Node selector constraints
Resource limitations
SCC violations
Issue 2: Permission Denied Errors
If you encounter permission errors:

oc auth can-i create virtualmachines --as=system:serviceaccount:openshift-cnv:kubevirt-controller
Verify service account permissions and SCC assignments.

Issue 3: API Server Not Responding
If virt-api is not responding:

oc get events -n openshift-cnv --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'
Check for certificate issues or network connectivity problems.

